  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1124 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nStrong southerly winds today will remain breezy tonight into \nSaturday ahead of an approaching cold front. Wind speeds tonight and \nSaturday will remain breezy, but much lower compared to this \nafternoon. This will result in seasonably mild conditions persisting \nover the region tonight into Saturday. A shortwave trough over the \nCentral Great Basin area today is progged to translate eastward into \nthe Rockies on Saturday before emerging over the Central Plains \nSaturday evening and Saturday night. A line of showers with embedded \nthunderstorms is expected to develop along a southeastward advancing \ncold front. The warm sector is progged to have dewpoints in the low \nto mid 50s with limited insolation due to widespread stratocu \nthrough the morning/early afternoon hours. This will likely limit \nheating leading to limited low-level buoyancy and storm potential. \nBut as the pv anomaly arrives on Saturday evening, mid-lvl lapse \nrates around 7 C/km combined with elevated CAPE of 200-300 J/KG may \nsupport some embedded thunderstorms along the advancing cold front \nthrough the evening hours before the activity races eastward as we \nmove through the overnight hours. \n\nA stable post-frontal regime will build over the region on Sun-Mon \nwith below average temperatures anticipated. A progressive shortwave \ntrough is progged to arrive over the central CONUS on Tue, but this \nis expected to have minimal impacts to sensible weather across our \nforecast area. Some increase in clouds could keep maximum \ntemperatures lower as we remain in a stable post-frontal airmass but \nprobabilities for meaningful precipitation are expected to remain \nlow at this time. We continue to see some large amplitude \ndifferences between the various models with this feature on Tue \nleading to low confidence at this time.\n\nShortwave ridging is progged to return to the central CONUS on Wed-\nFri, allowing temperatures to moderate with seasonably average \nvalues anticipated for late Nov. We could see another progressive \nshortwave trough towards the end of the week, but probabilities for \nmeaningful precipitation appear low at this time.",
